    <p class="eyebrow">Take action</p>
    <h1>Comment on the air permit</h1>
    <p class="lede">NJDEP is deciding whether to approve 12 diesel generators at 4405 Route 1. NJDEP says it will respond to every comment received on time. Your comment counts, and it doesn't need to be technical.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <div class="callout">
    <h2>How to comment</h2>
    <p data-until="2026-11-09"><strong>Comments are due by November 9, 2026.</strong></p>
    <p><a class="btn primary" href="https://dep.nj.gov/boss/permit-comment-form/" rel="noopener">NJDEP online comment form</a></p>
    <p>Or mail to: Nibras Mir, NJDEP, 401 E State Street, 2nd Floor, PO Box 420, Mail Code 401-02, Trenton, NJ 08625-0420.</p>
    <p>Either way, include this reference: <strong>"Draft Permit Comments for PN-DC1, PI 19525 PCP250001"</strong></p>
    <p class="muted small">Include your name and mailing or email address and NJDEP will send you its final decision. Source: <a href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-amended-public-notice-pcp250001.pdf">amended NJDEP public notice</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <h2>Five things to know</h2>
  <ul class="points30">
    <li><strong>No limit on emergency use.</strong> Testing is capped at 28 hours a year per engine. Emergency use isn't. <a class="cite" href="/south-brunswick/#m-hours">[details]</a></li>
    <li><strong>No one measures the pollution.</strong> The permit sets emission limits but requires no monitoring of them. <a class="cite" href="/south-brunswick/#m-monitor">[details]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Allowed on the worst days.</strong> They can run when the regional grid is strained, often during heat waves. Testing is banned on bad-air days; emergency use isn't. <a class="cite" href="/south-brunswick/#m-hours">[details]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Up to 900 lb of smog-forming NOx an hour.</strong> With all 12 running, as much as a 600 MW coal plant. <a class="cite" href="/south-brunswick/#m-coal">[details]</a></li>
    <li><strong>The health study isn't public.</strong> NJDEP says a health risk assessment found the risk "negligible," but it hasn't been released. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-amended-public-notice-pcp250001.pdf">[public notice]</a></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <h2>What to ask NJDEP for</h2>
  <p class="muted">Pick the ones that matter to you and say them in your own words. Personal comments carry more weight than form letters; a sentence about your own home and family makes yours stand out.</p>
  <ul class="asklist">
    <li><strong>Cap all operating hours,</strong> emergencies included, in the permit.</li>
    <li><strong>Count emergency use</strong> when calculating the facility's total pollution. EPA's own guidance uses 500 hours a year as a default, not 28. <a class="cite" href="https://www.epa.gov/sites/default/files/2015-08/documents/emgen.pdf" rel="noopener">[EPA, 1995]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Require emissions testing</strong> of the engines, and public yearly reports of how many hours they ran and why.</li>
    <li><strong>No running on bad-air days</strong> except true emergencies, and no use for grid programs or to cut the facility's electricity bills.</li>
    <li><strong>Release the health risk assessment,</strong> and show whether it modeled all 12 engines running at once during a long outage.</li>
    <li><strong>Disclose how much diesel will be stored</strong> on site and how many hours of operation it supports.</li>
    <li><strong>Explain why cleaner options weren't required:</strong> newer engines with exhaust controls, or batteries for short outages.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted small">Full details and permit section numbers: <a href="/south-brunswick/#math">how we calculated this</a> and <a href="/documents/">the draft permit and our key points</a>.</p>
